WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:03.811
Back to the move.

2
00:00:03.811 --> 00:00:10.021
A helper with a clipboard reads each box's label and writes new cards, which travel with the move.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:19.999
This time, the box with the @Builder note gets a builder: LampBuilder, a small class that collects a lamp's values one call at a time, then builds the lamp.

4
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:23.231
Here's a question many of us are too shy to ask.

5
00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:28.263
A generated file is just text, so why would anyone need a library to print it?

6
00:00:28.263 --> 00:00:30.457
Lesson 2's string template worked.

7
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:32.611
Let's try it.

8
00:00:32.611 --> 00:00:36.934
The template pastes each name and each type into a line of text.

9
00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:39.028
For Lamp, the builder compiles.

10
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:39.836
Now Load.

11
00:00:39.836 --> 00:00:45.487
It lives in package movemate.van, and its field is a Box, from package movemate.

12
00:00:45.487 --> 00:00:48.948
What happens when the build compiles its builder?

13
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.553
Unresolved reference Box: the template never wrote the import.

14
00:00:56.553 --> 00:01:04.557
And notice who complained: not KSP, but the Kotlin compiler, which reads a generated file exactly like yours.

15
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:07.394
The others slip the same way.

16
00:01:07.394 --> 00:01:10.119
Lamp's shade gets a second question mark.

17
00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:13.177
A nested Drawer must be called Kitchen.Drawer.

18
00:01:13.177 --> 00:01:17.099
And a property named in, a Kotlin keyword, needs backticks.

19
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:26.681
In the moving house, that's handwriting: a smudged mark, an abbreviation nobody explains, a forgotten room, and a word the reader takes as an order.

20
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:32.717
You could patch each slip by hand, until you notice you've written a small program that prints Kotlin.

21
00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:36.331
Good news: somebody already wrote it, and tested it far better.

22
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.896
So swap the handwriting for a label printer.

23
00:01:39.896 --> 00:01:45.778
You hand it parts, not letters: a class called LampBuilder, a field called name, of type String.

24
00:01:45.778 --> 00:01:51.599
It knows which names need an import, where the question marks go, and which words are keywords.

25
00:01:51.733 --> 00:01:52.684
Let's map it.

26
00:01:52.684 --> 00:01:56.265
The printer is KotlinPoet, a library from Square.

27
00:01:56.265 --> 00:02:04.233
Each part is a spec: FileSpec for a file, TypeSpec for a class, then PropertySpec, FunSpec and ParameterSpec.

28
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:10.887
Let's shrink it to one spec: a function called greet, which returns a String, Hello from Lamp.

29
00:02:10.887 --> 00:02:13.275
Before it prints, guess what comes out.

30
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:18.431
One line, with three habits.

31
00:02:18.431 --> 00:02:20.192
It wrote public by itself.

32
00:02:20.192 --> 00:02:23.716
It turned a one line return into an expression body.

33
00:02:23.716 --> 00:02:26.359
And alone, it spells out kotlin.String.

34
00:02:26.359 --> 00:02:29.815
Inside a file, that's plain String, plus an import.

35
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.582
Java has an older sibling, JavaPoet.

36
00:02:32.582 --> 00:02:39.827
A MethodSpec, a dollar sign where KotlinPoet has a percent sign, and the same greet as a Java method.

37
00:02:40.733 --> 00:02:47.461
That string, return %S, is a format string: code text with blanks, filled from the arguments after it.

38
00:02:47.461 --> 00:02:51.221
Four blanks, called placeholders, do nearly all the work.

39
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.462
%T takes a type.

40
00:02:52.462 --> 00:02:57.173
It prints the type's name, and puts the type on the file's import list.

41
00:02:57.333 --> 00:03:01.320
%N takes a name, and adds backticks when the name needs them.

42
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.131
Give it in, and out comes in, in backticks.

43
00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:10.929
%S takes a string, and writes a string literal: in quotes, with quotes and dollar signs escaped.

44
00:03:11.067 --> 00:03:15.127
And %L pastes a value as it is, no questions asked.

45
00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:19.235
That %S line looks like a cat walked across the keyboard.

46
00:03:19.235 --> 00:03:23.900
But Kotlin reads it back as exactly your text, dollar sign and all.

47
00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.938
Left bare, $room would quietly read a variable named room.

48
00:03:27.938 --> 00:03:33.299
So pass types with %T and names with %N, never pasted into the format string.

49
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.348
Now the whole builder.

50
00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:42.259
Watch the top of the file: every type that goes in through %T joins the import list.

51
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.756
FileSpec starts the file with its package, and TypeSpec adds the class.

52
00:03:47.756 --> 00:03:53.130
The first PropertySpec adds name, a String, and import kotlin.String appears.

53
00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.969
bulbs is an Int, so import kotlin.Int joins it, sorted.

54
00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:59.552
shade is a String again: nothing new.

55
00:03:59.733 --> 00:04:02.428
And shade has one question mark, not two.

56
00:04:02.428 --> 00:04:09.197
The type is made nullable with copy, nullable equals true, which leaves an already nullable type alone.

57
00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:14.992
Then a FunSpec per setter: each fills its slot and hands the builder back.

58
00:04:14.992 --> 00:04:16.982
A last FunSpec writes build.

59
00:04:16.982 --> 00:04:22.596
And writeTo hands the file to KSP, together with the box it came from, Lamp.kt.

60
00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:26.805
Now the one that failed: Load, in package movemate.van.

61
00:04:26.805 --> 00:04:33.467
Same specs, and the file starts with import movemate.Box, the line the handwriting forgot.

62
00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:35.169
Nobody typed an import.

63
00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:38.082
Here the picture breaks.

64
00:04:38.082 --> 00:04:41.112
A label printer prints exactly what you type.

65
00:04:41.112 --> 00:04:46.161
KotlinPoet edits as it prints: imports, public, backticks, a reshaped body.

66
00:04:46.161 --> 00:04:49.123
So read its output once before you trust it.

67
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.620
And it has a blind spot.

68
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:58.435
It describes declarations, not what happens inside a body: the body of build is just a format string.

69
00:04:58.435 --> 00:05:04.036
Fill name from the bulbs slot, and KotlinPoet prints that mistake, neatly indented.

70
00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.315
It's a printer, not a proofreader.

71
00:05:06.315 --> 00:05:12.661
The compiler reads for meaning, so every test in the lab compiles the generated builder, and calls it.

72
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.941
So, why a library to print text?

73
00:05:15.941 --> 00:05:20.559
Because generated code is ordinary source code, with a strict reader.

74
00:05:20.559 --> 00:05:28.121
Describe it as specs, pass types with %T and names with %N, and the printer writes the imports and the backticks.

75
00:05:28.121 --> 00:05:30.061
Then compile it, and call it.

76
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:30.973
Quick check.

77
00:05:30.973 --> 00:05:33.227
Crate has a property called object.

78
00:05:33.227 --> 00:05:36.769
Which placeholder carries its name, and what comes out?

79
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:40.242
%N.

80
00:05:40.242 --> 00:05:45.816
It prints object in backticks, because object is a keyword in Kotlin.
